Requires a second Ethernet interface
Laptop needs to remain physically connected to both networks

# 2. Ethernet → Laptop → Wi-Fi

This configuration is useful when you don't want to use two Ethernet cables.

## Diagram

```text
                    INTERNET
                       |
                       v
                +-------------+
                | Main Router |
                +------+------+
                       |
                   Ethernet
                       |
                       v
              +-----------------+
              |   OLD LAPTOP    |
              |                 |
              | WAN: Ethernet   |
              | LAN: Wi-Fi      |
              |                 |
              | NAT + Firewall  |
              | DHCP + DNS      |
              +--------+--------+
                       |
                     Wi-Fi
                  )))))))))))
                 ))    ))    ))
                PC    Phone  Laptop
```

## What you need

* 1 Ethernet cable
* Laptop's Ethernet port
* Laptop's Wi-Fi adapter

The laptop connects to your existing router through Ethernet and creates a new Wi-Fi network for your devices.

## Advantages

* Only one Ethernet cable
* No USB Ethernet adapter required
* Phones and laptops can connect wirelessly
* Good demonstration of a router and wireless access point

## Catch

* The laptop's Wi-Fi adapter must support Access Point (AP) mode
* Old Wi-Fi hardware may have poor performance
* Linux Wi-Fi drivers can sometimes be problematic
* Wi-Fi performance will probably be limited by the old hardware
* Laptop must remain powered on

## Best for

A convenient home experiment where you want your devices to connect wirelessly.

# 3. Wi-Fi → Laptop → Ethernet

Here the laptop gets Internet through Wi-Fi from your existing router.

## Diagram

```text
                    INTERNET
                       |
                       v
                +-------------+
                | Main Router |
                +------+------+
                       |
                     Wi-Fi
                  )))))))))))
                       |
                       v
              +-----------------+
              |   OLD LAPTOP    |
              |                 |
              | WAN: Wi-Fi      |
              | LAN: Ethernet   |
              |                 |
              | NAT + Firewall  |
              | DHCP + DNS      |
              +--------+--------+
                       |
                   Ethernet
                       |
                       v
                      PC
```

## Advantages

* Only one Ethernet cable
* No USB Ethernet adapter required
* Useful if you cannot run Ethernet from the main router

## Catch

* Wi-Fi becomes your WAN connection
* Potentially slower
* More latency
* Wi-Fi connection can be less reliable
* Linux Wi-Fi drivers can cause problems
* Your laptop's Wi-Fi is receiving the Internet rather than providing Wi-Fi to your devices

## Best for

Testing and experimentation rather than a permanent router.

# 4. Wi-Fi → Laptop → Wi-Fi

The laptop receives Internet over Wi-Fi and then creates another Wi-Fi network.

## Diagram

```text
                    INTERNET
                       |
                       v
                +-------------+
                | Main Router |
                +------+------+
                       |
                     Wi-Fi
                  )))))))))))
                       |
                       v
              +-----------------+
              |   OLD LAPTOP    |
              |                 |
              | WAN: Wi-Fi      |
              | LAN: Wi-Fi      |
              |                 |
              | NAT + Firewall  |
              | DHCP + DNS      |
              +--------+--------+
                       |
                     Wi-Fi
                  )))))))))))
                 ))    ))    ))
                PC    Phone  TV
```

## Advantages

* Zero Ethernet cables
* Completely wireless
* Interesting networking experiment

## Catch

* Most complicated configuration
* Wi-Fi adapter may not support the required modes
* Single Wi-Fi adapter has limitations
* Performance can be poor
* More difficult to troubleshoot
* Not recommended for your first setup

## Best for

Experimenting after you understand the basics.

# 5. Laptop Router + Separate Wi-Fi Access Point

This configuration is actually very common in real networks.

The laptop performs the routing while a separate device provides Wi-Fi.

## Diagram

```text
                    INTERNET
                       |
                       v
                +-------------+
                | Main Router |
                +------+------+
                       |
                   Ethernet
                       |
                       v
              +-----------------+
              |   OLD LAPTOP    |
              |                 |
              | WAN: Ethernet   |
              | LAN: Ethernet   |
              |                 |
              | NAT + Firewall  |
              | DHCP + DNS      |
              +--------+--------+
                       |
                   Ethernet
                       |
                       v
                +-------------+
                | Wi-Fi Access|
                |    Point    |
                +------+------+
                       |
                    Wi-Fi
                 )))))))))))
                Phone/Laptop/TV
```

## Advantages

* Very reliable
* Dedicated device handles Wi-Fi
* Laptop can focus entirely on routing
* Easy to troubleshoot
* Better Wi-Fi performance
* Similar to how many real networks are designed

## Catch

* Requires an additional Wi-Fi access point
* Usually requires two Ethernet connections to the laptop
* Additional hardware and cost




you can use whichever might be feasible.
